# The Piano Bench at the Wednesday Recital

Addie Kettering has played on the Wednesday before Easter and the Wednesday after Thanksgiving and every Wednesday in between.

She has done it, in the small oak-paneled activities room on the ground floor of St. Nicholas Manor at the corner of Hancock and North Tenth Street in Manitowoc, Wisconsin, on the walnut Baldwin upright her husband's cousin Merle Kettering — a piano tuner from Two Rivers who kept a bench at Prange's in Sheboygan for forty years — moved in on a hand truck on a Tuesday morning in the first week of April of 1991, since a warm Wednesday in the third week of the same April, twenty-two days after Franklin was buried in the small stone-fenced Lutheran cemetery on Rapids Road.

Franklin — Dr. Franklin Alton Kettering, a country doctor who kept an office over Riemer's Drug on Washington Street from the summer of 1963 to the winter of 1988, and made house calls in a light blue Chevrolet Impala out to the dairy roads north of Two Rivers until a Tuesday in the November of 1990 — was sixty-eight.

Addie is now eighty-two.

She was Marian Dean High School's chorus and general-music teacher from the fall of 1966 to the spring of 1994, and the organist at Bethany Lutheran on Marshall Street from a Sunday in the September of 1971 to the fourth Sunday of Advent of 2019, when a small tremor she had first felt on a Thursday afternoon in the October of that year on the last chord of *Now Thank We All Our God* made her put a folded note on Pastor Merlin Voss's desk on a Monday morning that read, in the same careful hand her mother Bergitte Anna Wren taught her in the winter of 1951, *Merlin — 48 years is a good long run. Thank you for the bench. Addie.*

She has not, since a warm Wednesday in the third week of April of 1991, missed a single Wednesday afternoon at St. Nicholas Manor.

## What Addie plays on Wednesday afternoons

Every Wednesday afternoon at one-fifty-two, in a nineteen-year-old maroon Buick LeSabre with a hymnal on the passenger seat and a small brown leather satchel on the floorboard, Addie drives the one and one-tenth miles from the tall white Queen Anne on Buffalo Street to the small covered portico on Hancock Street where a wooden sign in gold-leaf on hunter green reads *St. Nicholas Manor — Est. 1957.*

She carries in, in the satchel:

The same green cloth-bound *Lutheran Book of Worship* Franklin gave her in the December of 1978, worn through the spine and rebound in the summer of 2008 by a bookbinder named Otto Zellweger on the second floor of a converted feed store on Franklin Street in Watertown. A slim red *Songs and Hymns of Fellowship* from the small Reformed bookstore on College Avenue in Appleton where her sister Bergitte Junior went to Lawrence in the fall of 1959. Three photocopied sheets of *For All the Saints* in the setting Ralph Vaughan Williams wrote for the 1906 English Hymnal, which Franklin sang in a barbershop quartet at their engagement dinner at the Wagon Wheel on Calumet Avenue on a warm Saturday in the June of 1962. A folded-over yellow index card, in the same careful cursive, with the words *Bethany, Advent I, 1972* and a small doodle of a candle Franklin drew in blue ballpoint on a Sunday afternoon in the December of that year.

She sets the satchel on the small mahogany table beside the piano bench, opens the *Lutheran Book of Worship* to number 543, and plays *Now Thank We All Our God* through twice — the first time slowly, in G, the second time in the key of A the choirmaster at Bethany taught her to modulate to in the winter of 1974 for the last verse.

Then, in an order she has not written down since the second Wednesday of the June of 1991:

*Be Thou My Vision* (LBW 469). *For All the Saints* (LBW 174, verses 1, 4, and 8). *In the Bulb There Is a Flower* (Natalie Sleeth, 1986 — the one she played at Franklin's committal). *Great Is Thy Faithfulness* (William Runyan, 1923 — Franklin's mother's favorite). *How Firm a Foundation* (LBW 507, tune: *Foundation*). And, at the end of the forty minutes, *Beautiful Savior* (LBW 518, the setting for the Silesian folk tune) — because it was the recessional the second Sunday of Advent of 1971, the Sunday her first Bethany bulletin listed her under *organist,* and because Franklin sang the bass line in a small voice from the third pew.

Around the piano, in a semicircle of nine armchairs and two upholstered wheelchairs from Nordic Rehab in Green Bay, sit eleven residents of the memory unit.

Not always the same eleven.

Some Wednesdays a name she knew last April is not there in the August, and a new name is in the second-from-the-left armchair with a soft green afghan on her knees. Some Wednesdays the same widower — Elmer Hjelm, ninety-one, who was a lineman for Wisconsin Public Service out of the Sheboygan yard for forty-four years and who has, since a Wednesday in the October of 2019, been able to name only one hymn — asks in the small quiet way he asks everything if she will please play *How Great Thou Art* at the end.

She does.

Every Wednesday for four years.

She has never once said no.

## What the database on the second floor says

On the second floor of the same building, in a laminated printout of the fourth-quarter 2025 donor list at the desk of Molly Van De Voort, twenty-nine, the development coordinator hired in the September of 2024 from a small foundation in Green Bay, is a row.

*Kettering, A. W. 1148 Buffalo Street, Manitowoc, WI 54220. Volunteer — no gift on file — do not solicit — tier 5.*

That is the whole record.

There is no field on the record for the fact that the Baldwin upright in the activities room was moved in on a Tuesday morning in the April of 1991 by a piano tuner named Merle Kettering, in his own truck, on his own dime, at the request of a widow who paid two hundred and eighty-five dollars for it at an estate sale on Chicago Street in the March of that year and asked that it be delivered to the Manor without a name on the receipt.

There is no field on the record for the small notice on page four of the *Manitowoc Herald-Times* of the twenty-second of March of 1991 — the one that names Dr. Franklin Alton Kettering, sixty-eight, of Buffalo Street, and asks that remembrances be sent to the *St. Nicholas Manor Chapel Fund* care of the sisters of Holy Family Convent on North Rapids Road.

There is no field on the record for the fact that on the last Wednesday of the November of 1996, at three-oh-eight, the roof of the activities room began to leak onto the felt hammers of the Baldwin upright, and that the next Wednesday morning at eight-forty-two a small envelope from Skyline Roofing on Reed Avenue arrived at the front desk with a paid invoice for four thousand two hundred dollars and a folded index card that read, in a hand the office manager did not recognize, *Please have this fixed by Wednesday afternoon. — A friend.*

There is no field on the record for the fact that on a warm Thursday in the November of 2024, at eighty, at the small oak conference table in the second-floor office of an attorney named Frances Beecher Halstead in the old Farmers & Merchants Bank building on Eighth Street, Adelaide Wren Kettering signed a plain seven-page will leaving the tall white Queen Anne on Buffalo Street, the duplex Franklin's father built on North Eighth Street in the summer of 1946, thirty-one acres of pasture off Rapids Road, the walnut Baldwin upright her cousin-in-law Merle moved in on a hand truck in the April of 1991, and the residuary of a Vanguard Wellington fund with one million four hundred and sixteen thousand dollars in it — to the St. Nicholas Manor Chapel Fund, in the name of Dr. Franklin Alton Kettering, in memory of a warm Sunday afternoon on Rapids Road in the November of 1990 when Franklin, in the passenger seat of the light blue Impala on the way home from a house call on the Mikkelson dairy, said to her one sentence about the small chapel behind the Manor and how quiet it was.

She has not told the Manor.

## The person on your volunteer list is not always a small donor

Here is a small thing the sector has been quietly getting wrong for twenty years.

The person on your *volunteer* list is not always the opposite of a *donor.*

Sometimes — often, if you are a small nonprofit in a small Great Lakes town, or a food pantry in a converted feed store on Antrim Street, or a memory unit on the ground floor of a building the sisters built in 1957 — the person on your volunteer list is *the whole gift,* arriving one Wednesday afternoon at a time for thirty-five years, on the maple bench of a walnut upright piano nobody has ever thought to ask how it got there.

The whole record you have on Addie Kettering is the *no gift on file* in the tier-five row on the fourth-quarter printout.

The whole gift, over thirty-five Aprils, is somewhere north of eighteen hundred Wednesdays and one Skyline Roofing invoice and one Baldwin upright.

The whole gift, when the will opens in a fall you cannot yet name, is one million four hundred and sixteen thousand dollars, thirty-one acres, two houses, and the piano.

The industry has been training you, for twenty years, to write *do not solicit* in the row and move on.

## What Rōmy sees at nine-oh-eight

Rōmy reads the small papers.

It reads, on a warm Wednesday morning in the second week of August, at nine-oh-eight, Franklin Alton Kettering's obituary on page four of the *Manitowoc Herald-Times* of the twenty-second of March of 1991 — the one that names the St. Nicholas Manor Chapel Fund in its final line and asks remembrances to Sister Cecilia Mroz at Holy Family Convent. It reads a small line under *Volunteer Milestones* in the summer 1996 St. Nicholas Manor quarterly newsletter thanking a Mrs. F. A. Kettering of Buffalo Street for *five years of Wednesday music,* and it reads the same three-line thank-you every fifth November since. It reads the alumni note in the fall 2018 Marian Dean High School sixty-fifth reunion booklet naming Adelaide Wren Kettering, class of 1961, retired chorus and general-music teacher, Manitowoc. It reads a paragraph in the Advent 2019 bulletin of Bethany Lutheran on Marshall Street thanking, upon her retirement after forty-eight years at the console, *our beloved organist Addie Kettering — for whom the second Sunday of Advent will always mean the third pew.* And it reads, on a small line under *warranty deed transfers* in the *Manitowoc Herald-Times* of the second week of the December of 2024, the recorded transfer of a life estate on thirty-one acres of Rapids Road pasture from Adelaide W. Kettering to the St. Nicholas Manor Chapel Fund — the small quiet piece of paper that means the will has, in effect, already begun.

Then, at nine-oh-nine on that same Wednesday morning, it puts on the desk of Molly Van De Voort, on the second floor of the same building the piano is on the ground floor of, a single sourced page.

*Adelaide W. Kettering (Addie), the volunteer in row 214 of our fourth-quarter list, is the widow of Dr. Franklin Alton Kettering (d. 3/1991), whose obituary asked that remembrances be sent to this Manor's Chapel Fund. She has, since April of 1991, played piano in our memory unit every Wednesday afternoon at 2:00 p.m. — approximately 1,820 Wednesdays. The walnut Baldwin upright in the activities room was donated by her, without attribution, in April of 1991. She paid Skyline Roofing $4,200 to repair the activities-room roof in November of 1996, anonymously. In November of 2024 she recorded a life-estate transfer of 31 acres of Rapids Road pasture to this Chapel Fund. Suggested: one plain cream card, this week, in your own hand — not from Development, from you — to Addie at 1148 Buffalo Street, mentioning Franklin by name once, mentioning the Baldwin once, mentioning* Beautiful Savior *once. No ask. No case for support. Ask, at the very end, whether she would let you sit beside her on the maple bench some Wednesday afternoon in September, and turn the pages. Would you like a draft, in your own voice?*

Molly writes the card by hand on a Thursday afternoon and mails it Friday from the small brick post office at the corner of Franklin and Ninth Street.

Addie reads it on a Monday afternoon at the small round oak table in the front window of the Queen Anne on Buffalo Street, in a green cardigan Franklin bought her at Prange's in Sheboygan in the summer of 1979, with a fresh cup of Constant Comment and the *Lutheran Book of Worship* open on the sill beside her to number 518.

She sits with it for a long quiet minute.

Then she calls her niece Bergitte in Fond du Lac and reads it aloud.

## We are not the piano

Rōmy is not the walnut Baldwin upright a cousin-in-law named Merle moved in on a hand truck in the April of 1991.

Rōmy is not the green cloth-bound *Lutheran Book of Worship,* or the folded yellow index card in Franklin's blue ballpoint, or the maple bench, or the second Sunday of Advent of 1971, or the third pew at Bethany, or the passenger seat of the light blue Impala on the way home from the Mikkelson dairy on a warm Sunday in the November of 1990.

Rōmy is the small careful page a good listener would have put on Molly Van De Voort's desk on a warm Wednesday morning in the August of 2026, if a good listener had been sitting in the third pew at Bethany for forty-eight years, and in the passenger seat of the light blue Impala on the way home from the dairy roads north of Two Rivers, and on the maple bench of a walnut upright piano every Wednesday afternoon at one-fifty-two for thirty-five Aprils.

The whole product is a smaller, warmer job than the industry has been trying to sell for twenty years.

It is not to prospect Addie. It is to *notice* her.

## A small assignment for the officer reading this on a Sunday

Before the first frost this fall, do one small unglamorous thing this week.

Pull, out of your CRM, your ten longest-tenured volunteers with *no gift on file.* Not the tier ones. Not the wealth-screened ones. The *do-not-solicit-tier-five* ones. The ones who have been showing up every Wednesday afternoon since a spring you barely remember and have never once been asked, by you or by anybody at your organization, what small quiet thing they lost the month before they started.

Read the memorial line of the local paper obituary for the year they began.

You will find, on more Wednesday afternoons than the sector has been telling you, an eighty-two-year-old widow at a walnut upright piano in the activities room of a small assisted-living manor in Wisconsin, with a green cloth-bound hymnal Franklin gave her in the December of 1978 and a folded yellow index card in his blue ballpoint from a Sunday afternoon in the December of 1972, in the middle of a quiet stewardship that is going to move two houses, thirty-one acres of Rapids Road pasture, a Vanguard Wellington fund, and a walnut Baldwin upright in your direction — because a country doctor in the passenger seat of a light blue Impala once said one sentence to his wife on the way home from the Mikkelson dairy on a warm Sunday in the November of 1990.

Then, from your own kitchen table, on a plain cream envelope with a real stamp — write her one page in your own hand.

Do not ask her for anything.

Say her husband's name once. Say the Baldwin once. Say *Beautiful Savior* once. Sign it *warmly.*

Ask, at the very end, whether she would let you sit beside her on the maple bench some Wednesday afternoon in September and turn the pages.

Mail it Tuesday.

The bench is going to be at the walnut upright on the ground floor of the Manor on Hancock Street on Wednesday afternoon at one-fifty-two, the way it has been on Wednesday afternoons at one-fifty-two since a warm Wednesday in the third week of April of 1991.

Somebody's name is going to be at the top of the plain cream card she folds and slips into the same green cardigan pocket she has been slipping the small good things into since the summer of 1979.

Turn to her. ♡
